workedExamples.question_text:
Leah plays a hockey match and a rugby match. The results are independent. Her probability of winning hockey is $\frac{2}{5}$ and her probability of winning rugby is $\frac{3}{4}$. In the tree, $W$ means win and $L$ means lose.

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[x=1cm,y=1cm,draw=black,text=black,every node/.style={font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont},p/.style={fill=white,inner sep=2pt}]
\draw (0,0)--(2,1.5) node[midway,above,p] {$\frac{2}{5}$};
\draw (0,0)--(2,-1.5) node[midway,below,p] {$?$};
\path (2,1.5) node[p] {$W$} (2,-1.5) node[p] {$L$};
\draw (2.3,1.5)--(4.6,2.4) node[midway,above,p] {$?$};
\draw (2.3,1.5)--(4.6,0.6) node[midway,below,p] {$?$};
\draw (2.3,-1.5)--(4.6,-0.6) node[midway,above,p] {$?$};
\draw (2.3,-1.5)--(4.6,-2.4) node[midway,below,p] {$?$};
\path (4.6,2.4) node[right] {$W$} (4.6,0.6) node[right] {$L$} (4.6,-0.6) node[right] {$W$} (4.6,-2.4) node[right] {$L$};
\path (2,3.2) node {Hockey} (4.6,3.2) node {Rugby};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

(a) Complete the missing branch probabilities.
(b) Find the probabilities that Leah wins both matches, loses both matches, and wins hockey but loses rugby.
(c) Find the probability that she wins exactly one match. Give fractions in simplest form.

workedExamples.solution_text:
The hockey branches add to $1$. Independence means the rugby probabilities are the same after either hockey result.

$\begin{aligned}P(\text{lose hockey})&=1-\frac{2}{5}=\frac{3}{5}\\P(\text{lose rugby})&=1-\frac{3}{4}=\frac{1}{4}\end{aligned}$

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[x=1cm,y=1cm,draw=black,text=black,every node/.style={font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont},p/.style={fill=white,inner sep=2pt}]
\draw (0,0)--(2,1.5) node[midway,above,p] {$\frac{2}{5}$};
\draw (0,0)--(2,-1.5) node[midway,below,p] {$\frac{3}{5}$};
\path (2,1.5) node[p] {$W$} (2,-1.5) node[p] {$L$};
\draw (2.3,1.5)--(4.6,2.4) node[midway,above,p] {$\frac{3}{4}$};
\draw (2.3,1.5)--(4.6,0.6) node[midway,below,p] {$\frac{1}{4}$};
\draw (2.3,-1.5)--(4.6,-0.6) node[midway,above,p] {$\frac{3}{4}$};
\draw (2.3,-1.5)--(4.6,-2.4) node[midway,below,p] {$\frac{1}{4}$};
\path (4.6,2.4) node[right] {$W$} (4.6,0.6) node[right] {$L$} (4.6,-0.6) node[right] {$W$} (4.6,-2.4) node[right] {$L$};
\path (6.3,2.4) node {$WW:\ \frac{3}{10}$} (6.3,0.6) node {$WL:\ \frac{1}{10}$} (6.3,-0.6) node {$LW:\ \frac{9}{20}$} (6.3,-2.4) node {$LL:\ \frac{3}{20}$};
\path (2,3.2) node {Hockey} (4.6,3.2) node {Rugby} (6.3,3.2) node {Outcome};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

Multiply along each required path.

$\begin{aligned}P(WW)&=\frac{2}{5}\times\frac{3}{4}=\frac{3}{10}\\P(LL)&=\frac{3}{5}\times\frac{1}{4}=\frac{3}{20}\\P(WL)&=\frac{2}{5}\times\frac{1}{4}=\frac{1}{10}\end{aligned}$

Exactly one win includes $WL$ and $LW$. Add their probabilities.

$\begin{aligned}P(\text{exactly one win})&=\frac{2}{5}\times\frac{1}{4}+\frac{3}{5}\times\frac{3}{4}\\&=\frac{2}{20}+\frac{9}{20}\\&=\frac{11}{20}\end{aligned}$

